VoxCPM2: decode streaming chunks with rolling context, not from zero state

The streaming session hands the AudioVAE one patch at a time via
decode_features(patch, 1). That call is stateless -- it zero-fills its input
buffer, runs the graph and returns the samples -- so every chunk restarts the
decoder's causal convolutions from silence. The leading samples of each chunk
are decoded without their history, and each chunk boundary is audible as a
click. Measured on an 8 s utterance at 48 kHz: 19 sample discontinuities, every
one on a multiple of the 0.16 s patch period. The offline session, which decodes
the whole utterance in one graph, has none from the same weights and seed.

Keep the last three patches and decode them together with the current one, then
erase their samples before emitting. Every decoder convolution is causal, so
once the prefix covers the receptive field the retained tail matches a
whole-utterance decode. This is the prefix-and-trim that run_offline_request
already performs with generated.decode_trim_patches, and it matches the
reference implementation, whose streaming_prefix_len of 4 is three patches of
context plus the current one.

Cost is decoder work only: four patches per chunk instead of one. The LM and DiT
work per chunk is unchanged and dominates, so time to first audio is unaffected.
This also fixes the merged whole-utterance buffer, so plain non-streaming
requests to a streaming-mode model stop clicking too.

